- op: discuss  # ea54
  at: '2026-09-18T08:41:41Z'  # ea54
  by: agent  # ea54
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ea54
  clock: 3  # ea54
  nonce: ea54  # ea54
  message: "CAUTION ON THE CITED PRECEDENT, 2026-09-18. This row proposes a SECOND SCIP-style\nfidelity backend, and its title cites \"ADR-0012 coexistence, spec s20\" while the\nbody uses the shipped rust-analyzer arm as precedent. Both halves of that\nfoundation were measured this week and neither holds as cited.\n\n1. THE CITATION. ADR-0012 contains no SCIP ruling at all -- scip 0,\n   rust-analyzer 0, dedup 0 by term. Its two \"coexist\" uses are about analyzer\n   REGISTRATION systems and about two EDGES at one callsite. The spec asserts\n   backend coexistence as settled behaviour citing \"ADR-0012 coexistence\", and\n   docs/future/scip-generalization-vision.md lists coexistence-vs-replacement as\n   an OPEN question. Filed as its own needs_human_review row (2026-09-18).\n\n2. THE PRECEDENT. Measured on aardvark-dns at d6174d5df9 with the backend active:\n     - cross-backend stable_id parity: 0 of 52 overlapping symbols\n     - cross-pass symbol dedup: never implemented (git log -S over full history)\n     - 500 of 669 SCIP nodes are function-locals named by a document-scoped\n       index; 329 of 455 local-pointing edges are provably false cross-file\n       references, 21.2% of every edge in the artifact\n   So \"rust-analyzer shipped and coexists cleanly\" is not what shipped.\n\n3. THE VALUE PROPOSITION HAS A MEASURED COUNTEREXAMPLE IN RUST, AND IT IS THIS\n   ROW'S OWN PROPOSITION. This row is titled \"receiver typing\". Two-arm diff on\n   aardvark-dns, default vs backend-on: **453 call edges in BOTH arms, 0\n   differing, 0 receivers typed differently** (WI-jivim, corrected 2026-09-17\n   after I had wrongly told the owner the backend out-produced tree-sitter -- all\n   637 of its edges are `references` and ZERO are call-family). The one language\n   where a fidelity backend has actually shipped produced no receiver typing at\n   all.\n\n   That does NOT prove pyright would behave the same way -- SCIP-python and\n   rust-analyzer are different indexers with different occurrence coverage, and\n   the Rust arm's zero may be an artifact of how its edges are built rather than\n   of the fidelity idea. But it does mean \"already shipped for Rust\" cannot be\n   offered as evidence that the approach converts into typed receivers. It is\n   the opposite of evidence.\n\nNOT ASKING FOR THIS ROW TO CLOSE. The multi-fidelity goal may well be right, and\nspec s20 still lists it. What this note asks is that the row not be started on\nthe strength of a precedent that measures zero, and that whoever picks it up\nfirst answers: what would pyright give us that rust-analyzer measurably did not?\nA cheap way to answer it is a two-arm diff on a Python repo before any wrapper is\nbuilt -- the reverse of the Rust order, which built the wrapper first\n(docs/future/scip-generalization-vision.md's own Open Question 1 raises exactly\nthat ordering doubt)."  # ea54
